sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch05-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In naked protoplasmic cells the amoeboid form marks a phase of activity with unequal surface tensions, whereas the spherical form marks a phase of rest when internal homogeneity and minimal potential energy are attained, at which time the cell also becomes encysted.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Resting cells are spherical; the amoeboid phase is one of activity and unequal tensions";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter V, “The Forms of Cells”; topic “Surface tension in protoplasm and amoeba”. Scope: naked protoplasmic cells, amoebae and other protists with resting stages"
}
